  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes phototherapy for cancer, combining how treatment is delivered, the underlying light-driven mechanism, and several illumination modes. Overall, it depicts a patient receiving a light-activated therapeutic agent that accumulates in tumor tissue, after which the tumor is exposed to light and then reduced or eliminated.\n\nThe top schematic shows the treatment sequence: a tumor-bearing patient is injected with a photosensitive agent, the agent appears to distribute through the body and then concentrate more strongly in the tumor, and the tumor is subsequently illuminated, leading to treatment and an apparently tumor-free outcome. This conveys the basic workflow of image-guided or targeted phototherapy.\n\nThe central diagram explains the photophysical basis of PTT/PDT. Light excites the agent from a ground state to an excited singlet state. From there, one pathway relaxes vibrationally to produce heat, representing photothermal therapy (PTT), while another undergoes intersystem crossing to generate reactive oxygen species (ROS), representing photodynamic therapy (PDT). Fluorescence is also indicated as an additional de-excitation route.\n\nThe bottom row highlights different light-delivery strategies, ranging from surface and interstitial fiber irradiation to endoscopic balloon-catheter approaches, broader external field treatment, and even daylight exposure, suggesting adaptability to different lesion locations and clinical contexts.",
